.\" cantriple-socketcan(7)
.TH CANTRIPLE\-SOCKETCAN 7 "October 2026" "cantriple-socketcan 1.0.0" "Miscellaneous"
.SH NAME
cantriple\-socketcan \- the CAN Triple 2.0 as a SocketCAN adapter: setup, settings and behaviour
.SH DESCRIPTION
The CAN Triple 2.0 can present itself to a PC as a gs_usb CAN adapter, the
protocol of the Linux kernel's own
.B gs_usb
driver. Its three buses then become SocketCAN network interfaces with no
driver to build or install; the unit applies the bit timing Linux computes,
reports its bus state and errors as SocketCAN expects, timestamps every frame
in hardware, and confirms each frame it sends only once the frame is on the
wire. This package adds the glue: a udev rule that binds
.B gs_usb
to the unit and names its interfaces, an optional bring-up at set bit rates,
and
.BR ctriple (1).
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.SS The unit
A CAN Triple 2.0. Its firmware decides what is there:
.TP
.B 2.0.7
SocketCAN mode: classic CAN at any bit rate, normal, listen-only and loopback,
frames both ways, echoes after transmission.
.TP
.B 2.0.8
Hardware timestamps, bus state and error counters, bus-off reporting and
recovery, bus-error reports, the standard's fault confinement.
.TP
.B 2.0.9
CAN FD, data phase to 8 Mbit/s.
.TP
.B 2.0.10
The mode kept across power-ups, the fallback to the Device Manager's port, and
the mode's report
.RB ( "ctriple status" ).
.PP
Update the unit with the Device Manager's
.BR "Update Firmware" .
.SS The PC
Linux with
.BR gs_usb ,
udev,
.BR ip (8)
and Python 3 with
.BR python3\-serial .
.B gs_usb
in Linux 6.2 or later uses everything the unit offers; 6.6 (a long-term
release) or later is recommended. 5.18 to 6.1 have the three channels, CAN FD
and hardware timestamps, but no error counters in
.B "ip \-details"
and no bus-error reports. Before 5.18 the driver cannot use the unit.
.B can\-utils
(candump, cansend, cangen, canbusload) is recommended.
.SH QUICK START
.nf
.RS
sudo apt install ./cantriple\-socketcan_1.0.0_all.deb
ctriple socketcan
sudo ctriple up \-\-bitrate 500000
candump ct4f002b\-can1
cansend ct4f002b\-can1 123#DEADBEEF
ctriple manager
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B ctriple socketcan
prints the unit's interface names; ct4f002b is the unit of this example.
.SH THE TWO MODES
A unit is the Device Manager's port or the SocketCAN adapter, never both at
once.
.TP
.B The Device Manager's port
A USB serial port (0483:5740, product "CAN Triple 2.0"), the Device Manager's
protocol on it. The unit runs its stored configuration; every Device Manager
function works.
.TP
.B The SocketCAN adapter
One vendor-class USB interface (0483:5740, product "CAN Triple 2.0
SocketCAN") with two bulk endpoints, which
.B gs_usb
takes. There is no serial port: the Device Manager cannot see the unit until
it is the Manager's port again.
.PP
.B ctriple socketcan
and
.B ctriple manager
switch; each switch takes the unit off the USB bus for a quarter of a second,
and the PC then finds the other device. The unit's own configuration runs on
through a switch, in both modes; nothing about a switch is a restart.
.SS Kept across power-ups
With
.BR \-\-persist ,
the mode is also the one the unit starts in at every power-up, kept in its
FRAM beside (not in) its device settings, so a Device Manager that knows
nothing of it cannot clear it. Without it, a unit always starts as the
Manager's port. The kept mode is reported by
.BR "ctriple status" .
.SS FALLBACK
An adapter needs a PC that can use one, and the Device Manager must always be
able to reach the unit. So while the unit is the adapter, whether it started
as one or was asked to be one, it watches each PC that enumerates it, from the
PC's first request:
.IP \(bu 2
not configured within 5 seconds (as Windows leaves a device it has no driver
for), or
.IP \(bu 2
configured, but not claimed by a
.B gs_usb
driver's first request within 60 seconds of that (a Linux PC without this
package's rule, or with a driver bound to the ID that is not
.BR gs_usb ;
the 60 seconds are for a PC whose udev runs late in its boot):
.PP
and the unit becomes the Manager's port for that PC.
.B ctriple status
then says "fell back from the SocketCAN adapter". When the USB cable comes
out, the next PC finds the adapter again.
.PP
The PC a mode was chosen on
.RB ( "ctriple socketcan" " or " manager ,
or the Device Manager's
.BR "Online \(-> USB Mode" )
is not watched until the cable comes out: it chose. A choice made on a unit
that fell back replaces the fallback, so a unit told there to keep the
Manager's port stays it when the cable comes out. Firmware before 2.0.11
watches only a unit that started as the adapter, and a host's choice there
ends the watch until the next power-up: a unit made the adapter on one PC and
carried, still powered, to one that cannot use it stays the adapter until its
power is cycled.
.SH INTERFACES
.SS Names
The udev rule names a unit's interfaces
.BI ct UNIT \-can1\fR,
.BI \-can2
and
.BI \-can3
after its CAN1, CAN2 and CAN3:
.I UNIT
is the last six characters of its serial number (its MCU ID) in lower case. The
same unit has the same names on every PC and every plug-in, and two units never
share them. The bus is also the interface's
.I dev_port
(0 to 2) in
.IR /sys/class/net/ .
Without the rule the kernel names them
.BR can0 ,
.BR can1 ,
\&... in the order it finds them.
.SS Bit timing
The unit offers the kernel its CAN controller's ranges and applies whatever
timing the kernel then computes, both phases, as given:
.PP
.TS
l l l.
	nominal	data
clock	80 MHz	80 MHz
tseg1	2 to 256	1 to 32
tseg2	2 to 128	1 to 16
sjw	1 to 128	1 to 16
brp	1 to 512	1 to 32
.TE
.PP
tseg1 is prop-seg plus phase-seg1 and tseg2 is phase-seg2, in time quanta; brp
is the clock's divider to one quantum; sjw can be no more than tseg2.
.PP
Every
.BR ip\-link (8)
form of a timing works:
.BR bitrate " and " sample\-point ,
or
.BR tq ", " prop\-seg ", " phase\-seg1 ", " phase\-seg2 " and " sjw ,
and for the data phase
.BR dbitrate " and " dsample\-point ,
or
.BR dtq ", " dprop\-seg ", " dphase\-seg1 ", " dphase\-seg2 " and " dsjw .
A bit rate can only be changed while the interface is down. The unit sets its
transmitter delay compensation itself from the data phase; Linux has no setting for it on this driver.
.SS Modes
.TP
.B fd on
ISO CAN FD (with a
.BR dbitrate ).
Each frame says whether it switches bit rate
.RB ( CANFD_BRS ).
Classic frames still work on an FD bus.
.TP
.B listen\-only on
Receive only: never acknowledge, transmit or send an error frame.
.TP
.B loopback on
Inside the controller: frames sent come back as received ones and the pins
stay recessive.
.TP
.B berr\-reporting on
Bus errors as error frames (see
.BR ERRORS ).
.PP
Not supported, and refused when asked for:
.BR one\-shot ,
.BR triple\-sampling ,
.BR fd\-non\-iso ,
.BR presume\-ack ,
.BR cc\-len8\-dlc .
.B termination
is not offered: board revision 2.00 cannot switch its 120 \(*W resistors at all
(fit them externally), and switching them from Linux on later boards is still
to come. Do not use
.B restart
or
.BR restart\-ms :
the unit recovers from bus-off itself, and
.B gs_usb
has no restart of its own (some kernels refuse it, some mishandle it).
.SS While Linux has a bus
A bus brought up from Linux runs Linux's bit rates and mode, in place of the
setup in the unit's configuration. The configuration keeps running: its
transmit messages still go out, among Linux's frames (Linux's go first), and
its receive messages are still decoded. When the interface goes down, or the
PC goes (the cable comes out, the PC re-enumerates the unit, or it sleeps or
shuts down with its port still powered), or the unit becomes the Manager's
port again, the bus returns to the
configuration's setup at once. Nothing set from Linux is stored on the unit.
The termination stays as the configuration sets it.
.SS Frames
.IP \(bu 2
Classic and CAN FD data frames both ways, 11- and 29-bit identifiers, every
length (0 to 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 32, 48, 64 bytes). Remote frames can be sent;
the unit does not deliver received remote frames.
.IP \(bu 2
A frame sent is confirmed to its sender (the SocketCAN echo, and what
.B candump
on the same interface shows as sent) only once the controller has transmitted
it. A frame nothing acknowledges is retried by the controller until something
does or the interface goes down; ten frames a bus can wait at once, after which
the interface's queue stops, as with any adapter on a bus nobody acknowledges.
.IP \(bu 2
Frames are timestamped in hardware: see
.BR TIMESTAMPS .
.SH TIMESTAMPS
The unit stamps every frame from its own 1 MHz clock (from its crystal): a
received frame in the interrupt that takes it from the controller, a sent frame
in the interrupt that sees it go, an error frame when it is made. The kernel
reads the clock to tie it to its own and offers the stamps as hardware
timestamps
.RB ( SO_TIMESTAMPING ;
.B "ethtool \-T"
lists hardware transmit and receive).
.B "candump \-H"
shows them. Against an independent analyser on the same bus, the gaps between
the unit's stamps of the same frames agree to about a microsecond.
.SH ERRORS
.SS State
The unit's state (error-active, warning, passive, bus-off) and its error
counters are what
.B "ip \-details link show"
reports, and each change is an error frame
.RB ( "candump \-e" ):
.BR CAN_ERR_CRTL
with
.B TX_WARNING / RX_WARNING / TX_PASSIVE / RX_PASSIVE / ACTIVE
and the counters, or
.BR CAN_ERR_BUSOFF ,
or
.B CAN_ERR_RESTARTED
when a bus that was off is back.
.B "ip \-details \-statistics"
counts them under
.BR error\-warn ", " error\-pass ", " bus\-off " and " re\-started .
.SS Fault confinement
On a bus Linux has, the unit keeps the standard's: a node nothing acknowledges
goes error passive (transmit counter 128) and stays there, retrying, as every
other adapter does. Bus-off is the controller's, and the unit brings the bus
back itself a second later, which Linux sees as restarted. The error counters
survive taking an interface down and up (the controller keeps them); a bus-off
recovery clears them.
.SS Bus errors
With
.BR "berr\-reporting on" ,
each kind of protocol error is an error frame as the kernel's own m_can driver
makes it
.RB ( CAN_ERR_PROT " | " CAN_ERR_BUSERROR ,
stuff, form, bit 0, bit 1 in data[2], CRC in data[3],
.B CAN_ERR_ACK
for an acknowledgement error), the latest one at most every millisecond a bus,
with the counters.
.SS Lost frames
A received frame the unit cannot pass to the PC in time is dropped, never
silently: the next frame delivered says frames were lost, which the kernel
counts as
.B overrun
.RB ( "ip \-s \-s link show" )
and reports as a
.B CAN_ERR_CRTL_RX_OVERFLOW
error frame. A frame sent is never dropped.
.SH THROUGHPUT
The unit is a full-speed USB device and the gs_usb protocol carries one frame
per USB transfer, so the three buses and the confirmations of sent frames
share about 13,000 to 14,000 transfers a second, whatever the bit rates. On
the bench, at 500 kbit/s with all three buses at full load in both directions
at once, about 3 % of received frames were dropped (and reported, see
.BR "Lost frames" );
below the limit every frame is carried. A bus's share of it is its frame rate,
not its bit rate: short frames at a high bit rate reach it soonest.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I /etc/default/cantriple\-socketcan
is read by
.B "ctriple up"
when it is given no bit rate, and by the automatic bring-up. It is a shell
fragment of
.IB NAME = value
lines:
.TP
.BR AUTO_UP= no | yes
.B yes
brings every bus that has a bit rate up by itself whenever the unit appears in
SocketCAN mode (at a switch, a plug-in, or a power-up that starts as the
adapter). Off by default: a bus brought up at the wrong bit rate puts error
frames on everything connected to it.
.TP
.BI BITRATE= N
Every bus's nominal bit rate, in bit/s.
.TP
.BI DBITRATE= N
Every bus's CAN FD data phase bit rate; empty for classic CAN.
.TP
.BI CAN1_BITRATE= N "\fR, " CAN1_DBITRATE= N "\fR, " CAN2_... "\fR, " CAN3_...
The same for one bus, in place of the two above. A bus left without a bit rate
is left down.
.TP
.BI OPTIONS= words
More
.B "ip link set \fIinterface\fP type can"
words for every bus, e.g.
.B
listen\-only on
or
.BR "berr\-reporting on" .
.PP
The file is a configuration file of the package: an upgrade keeps your edits.
.SH UDEV RULES
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-cantriple\-gs\-usb.rules
(a file of the same name in
.I /etc/udev/rules.d
replaces it):
.IP \(bu 2
loads
.B gs_usb
and gives it the unit's USB ID, once, when the unit appears as the adapter
(matching only a vendor-class interface of 0483:5740 whose manufacturer is
"Minton Performance"; the kernel then binds every later plug-in itself);
.IP \(bu 2
names each interface after the unit and the bus, then runs
.I /usr/lib/cantriple\-socketcan/ctriple\-ifup
for it, which does nothing unless
.BR AUTO_UP=yes ;
.IP \(bu 2
gives the user at the seat write access to the unit's USB node
.RB ( uaccess ),
for
.BR "ctriple manager" ;
.IP \(bu 2
tells ModemManager to leave the Device Manager's port alone.
.PP
The unit shares ST's USB ID 0483:5740 with other STM32 serial ports for now,
which is why the kernel has to be told the ID and why every match also checks
the manufacturer. With an ID of its own, the kernel's
.B gs_usb
table will name the unit and only the names will be left to the rule.
.SH THE CAN VIEWER PASSWORD
A unit with a CAN Viewer password (set in the Device Manager) does not become
the adapter until it is proved:
.B ctriple
asks for it, or takes
.B \-\-password
or
.BR CTRIPLE_PASSWORD .
The proof is a challenge and response; the password never crosses the cable.
Going back to the Manager's port needs no password: it only ever gives the
buses back.
.SH OTHER SYSTEMS
Windows and macOS have no gs_usb driver for the adapter; the fallback gives
such a PC the Device Manager's port.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.TP
.B "No interfaces after ctriple socketcan"
The rule is not installed, or a file of the same name in
.I /etc/udev/rules.d
masks it;
.B "lsmod | grep gs_usb"
and
.B dmesg
show whether the driver bound
("gs_usb ...: Configuring for 3 interfaces").
.TP
.BR "Interfaces named can" N
The rule did not name them: check as above.
.TP
.B "RTNETLINK answers: Device or resource busy"
A bit rate cannot be changed while the interface is up: take it down first
.RB ( "ctriple up"
does).
.TP
.B "ERROR-PASSIVE, transmit counter 128, nothing received"
Nothing on the bus acknowledges: no other node, a node at another bit rate, or
the wiring (CAN_H, CAN_L and ground; 120 \(*W at each end of the bus).
.TP
.B "The unit came up as the Manager's port"
It was not kept as the adapter, or it fell back
.RB ( "ctriple status"
says which): this PC did not configure it, or
.B gs_usb
did not claim it, in time. Unplug and plug in again once the rule is in place.
.TP
.B "Back to the Device Manager from Windows"
Plug the unit in: Windows has no driver for the adapter, so within a few
seconds the unit gives it the Manager's port (firmware 2.0.11 for a unit made
the adapter without
.BR \-\-persist ).
Then in the Device Manager,
.B "Online \(-> USB Mode"
and the Device Manager's port, to keep it so.
.TP
.B "The Device Manager cannot find the unit"
The unit is the adapter:
.BR "ctriple manager" .
.TP
.B "Frames dropped under heavy load"
See
.BR THROUGHPUT ;
each drop is reported as an overrun.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/ctriple
The command.
.TP
.I /etc/default/cantriple\-socketcan
The bit rates and the automatic bring-up.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-cantriple\-gs\-usb.rules
The binding, the names, the bring-up, the permissions.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/cantriple\-socketcan/ctriple\-ifup
The automatic bring-up, run by udev for each interface.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ctriple (1),
.BR ip\-link (8),
.BR candump (1),
.BR cansend (1),
.BR cangen (1),
.BR udev (7)
